A||||16||Nashco||This control arm bolt is killing me!!!!!!!!!  Any suggestions?||||1||
Z||000000||Nashco||09-22-2001||02:39 AM||||Boy oh boy...I'm so frustrated!  I'm currently installing poly bushings, balljoints, konis, and 1" springs up front..well I'm trying to anyway.  I have an extra set of control arms all ready to bolt in, just have to remove the stock parts.  So last tuesday I started removing stuff.  I got the whole right side removed and began tearing apart the left side.  I sprayed oil on all the nuts and bolts a couple days ahead of time, being careful and preparing myself well.  <P>So the next day I started taking apart the left side.  I got everything alright, except the two lower control arm bolts.  The right side had been sticky, but I was able to grease up and pound out the bolts.  The left side is just stuck!  I finally grunted hard enough to break the sleeve loose from the rubber, but that's not what I wanted!  I researched all I could about stuff people had gone through trying to remove these bolts...what a PITA!  Seems like GM should have thought this over a little better..heck, 2 cents worth of anti-sieze could make this a whole new ball game!<P>Anyway, so I tried (I think Ogre's method) using a cold punch and splitting the seams open on the sleeves.  Only one of them split open, but I still couldn't get it to do anything (the rear bolt).  The front wouldn't do anything, so I just sprayed a bunch more oil on it, dumped some special rust pentrant on it, and let it sit a few more days.  Today I go out to beat on it some more, but still can't split the seems enough to loosen anything.  I broke down and went and rented a sawzall and 10 blades from the home depot, intent on getting this f'r apart tonight.  So, after much mangling of the control arm mounting points (they look like crap now...that's gonna take a while to straighten out), I managed to cut the front bolt up and free it from the car, and cut most of the rear part of the rear bolt off.  I was expecting to finish real soon now, and got all excited.  I started sawing on the forward section of the sleeve, trying to cut through it.  Well, after dulling a new blade...I check to see how much damage I did.  Apparently the sleeve is MUCH harder than my saw blades, because I didn't make a scratch in it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><B>So, here's my plea</B>....does anybody have any idea what I should do now???  I don't think I can fit an electric grinder in there, but if somebody has used a 4" grinder and it fit, please let me know!  A die grinder/cut off tool would work perfectly for this, but I can't rent the tool, and there's no cheap tool places around here!  I don't have a small torch, and can't really afford to get one if it won't do the job anyway.  I'm renting tools from home depot, and the sawzall has to be back by 9am tomorrow (today..).  Any ideas...or somebody around here that I can borrow from?<P>Recap...tried:<BR>Tons of oil/rust penetrant for several days<BR>Split seams of sleeves (can't seem to accomplish much)<BR>Sawzall<P><B>Think I need</B>:<BR>Torch to heat up the outer sleeve enough to loosen the bolt inside it, but still unsure if this will work, or..<BR>Air compressor and die grinder (have truck, will travel, and make it up to you with a few bucks or beers..whatever)<P>Please...somebody...help me out here!  I have beat on the car until my back won't let me anymore (that's a lot, when you're 19  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), and I need to have this done this weekend!!!  I leave school for Alabama next weekend, and I was making it my goal to have it done by tomorrow night.  Phew...I'm so upset...haha..damn car is getting the best of me, that's not supposed to happen to me!:P<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-22-2001||06:01 AM||||Hey Bryce!<BR>Are you up here in Michigan yet?<BR>I just did my 86, and the drivers side was rusted like yours...<BR>I used a standard propane torch to burn out the rubber bushings, wear goggles!<BR>Then I used different size screw drivers to open the seams, then the bolts would pound out...<P><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||09-22-2001||06:34 AM||||Yeah, I'm in Flint right now, for another week only.<P>I was looking at the sleeves that were on the old control arms...I'm really surprised that you guys have been able to spread them; the sleeves are really thick, and super hard!  In frustration I went out and wasted a couple sawzall blades on one...didn't even phase it.<P>On your control arms, did the sleeves have a tight seam?  Mine is spread slightly in the front half, and none in the rear half.  I think that I'm not able to spread the sleeve because the rubber is forcing back on it...thus the burning of the rubber.  Didn't burning it make a HUGE mess...especially with your inner fender, coolant line, and brake line?  I think if I can get the rubber loose by burning it or what not, and spreading the sleeve, I could get the bolt out; my dilemma is that I can't get hardly any force on it in the direction of the sleeve.  The frame drops right behind the end of the bolt, and the coolant line and brake line are in the way if I wanted to drive the bolt out.  How'd you get past this?<P>I don't want to have to cut that sleeve because it'll take forever, but I also don't want to have to buy a propane torch just for this...I have very little cash and I have to move next week (with the fiero only) to Alabama.  Thanks for your help so far Fierowrecker...I'm staying alert hoping to get some more info, and perhaps somebody will let me borrow a tool.  I already borrowed a picklefork from Tigger last week, so I don't want to bother him with this, even though he is in Flint.<P>Keep the info rollin' <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Banzai||09-22-2001||10:06 AM||||Get the Tungsten Carbide coated blades for cutting tile and masonry. I used one blade to cut through the sleeves and bolts for the forward cradle mounts(four cuts). The blade is still good. I think the blade was about $6, compared to a pack of metal cutting blades at $12. IF YOU GET THIS BLADE YOU WILL SUCCEED.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||09-22-2001||02:12 PM||||I used a 4" cutoff wheel on a grinder and it worked perfectly. When I first started it didn't occur to me that you can grind it from any angle, I was very insistant that I hit the bolt from the side and cut it straight off. Once it hit me I decided to grind from the head tword the contol arm, and it worked perfectly. Occasionally you have to turn the bolt a little to get it cut off on all sides, but it worked. Took me about 30-45 minutes of work my first time, about 15 minutes on my second. I used an electric 5/6" grinder, and slapped a 4" wheel on it because any bigger wouldn't fit. I tried sawing on it, but I too found out that sleeve is pretty stong, actually the bolt it too, but the grinder takes care of it much better. Definatly reccomend you use a grinder, its alot less work then you'd think.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto, getting ready to install T-6 turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||09-22-2001||04:44 PM||||Alright guys, here's my decision, I think...<P>I'm not hip to the burning method at all...that sounds like a lot of unfun and pretty nasty, not to mention the fire potential...which wouldn't do much for my fiero's image.<P>I want to use the 4" grinder idea I think.  I originally was going to get a grinder, but wasn't sure how wellone would fit.  Now that I have the right side back together, I can bolt the wheel on and the car will be more stable, so I can lift the left side high up and get the arm out of the way for grinding.  Hopefully they rent a grinder there at home depot too...otherwise I'll take plan B.<P>Plan B is to get the sawzall again, this time getting the masonry tungsten carbide bit, as Banzai suggested.  If they cut the other sleeves and bolts and the blade was still alive, I think it'd get the job done for one of the control arm bolts.  even if I could only get halfway, I still have three regular blades left to finish it off...just going to be a pain to get started.  I figure a grinder should cost about the same as the sawzall to rent if they've got it, about 10 bucks for the night at home depot.  The cutting wheel should be a few bucks, and the blade is about 6 Banzai says, so I should be able to finish this mother off with less than 20 dollars invested.  <P>I also need to find some hardware.  Obviously I need new bolts.  I got 1/2" stuff because it looked like it'd work, but it's just barely too big.  They didn't have jack for metrics at home depot, so I'll try Lowe's and Autozone.....hopefully I can get the bolts, that'd really piss me off if I had to wait until the week to go to a real hardware nuts/bolts store <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Wish me luck guys....I'm going to be up late tonight trying to get this finished up and all installed.<P>Quick side note, since you've done this before too and you might now....do I use the same torque specs on all the control arm bolts as stock suggests?  I've got the 88 manual, so I have the specs, just need to look them up.<P>Thanks a ton everybody...I'm almost confident in myself again <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||qwikgta||09-22-2001||11:24 PM||||WOW..Nashco...I was in the garage today, got my sawzall from home depot two, and went to town on my 88 front suspension.  I too am putting new KYB's and Poly on all four corners.  I didnt get that far, I stopped after both sides were free of the springs.  I was going to tackle removing the lower control arms tomorrow, now I am scared.  I reread your threads, did you have trouble removing the bolts that held on the control arm to the frame.  If so that is what I was going to do tomorrow.  It looks like four bolts (two per side) did they give you that much trouble, maybe I should stop now.  Great...now what to do.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||09-22-2001||11:42 PM||||Nachco,<P>  Get the hardware from Mid-State Bolt and Screw.  They are located on the South-east corner of I-475 and Bristol Rd.  Unfortunately I doubt they're open tomorrow.<P>They have metric and factory 10.9 POR hardness bolts at almost any 10mm increment length.  Mid-State was the only place I could find replacements for my '84 and it only cost me about $8.00 for 4 bolts and nuts.  A dealer around here wanted about $10 a pop for each bolt!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>I didn't have much luck with a sawzall at all but I'd try the blade Banzai recommended.  I used a 4" wheel on a air die grinder to cut the bolts.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||09-23-2001||01:22 AM||||Well, status update...<P>I went to Lowe's and got some 7/16" bolts for a temporary fit.  I will get the hardware from a nut and bolt store on Tuesday when I have time and they're open.  The 7/16" should be about a mm too small, but they'll do for a day or two (I think <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).<P>I rented an electric grinder from Home Depot tonight (another 11 bucks).  I have it until tomorrow am, so the bolt will be out by then <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">!  I was going to get a super duper disk, but the guy at the rental counter just gave me the standard 4" metal disk, so I figured I'll stick with it.  Worst case scenario I'll have to go buy another one first thing tomorrow morning, best case I save a few bucks.  I'm about to go tackle that bolt head now..just got back from a little shindig and I'm kinda bored...hoping to be able to drive it to Lansing tomorrow <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Not required, but I would definitely like to.<P><B>qwikgta</B>:  The upper bolts were easy.  They had zero corrosion, and a fancy shmancy nut plate holding it all together.  The lowers are a real pain.  I'd spray everything heavily with oil and pray that some will come out easily.  If you think it'll be a ton of work..why not get started now though...don't stop <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P><B>Tigger</B>:  I'll get my hardware from them.  I was actually going to ask you tomorrow where I could get it.  I have been saying this whole time that a die grinder would have had this job done a week ago...arg!  I'll get your picklefork to tomorrow, hopefully.  If, for some reason, things go awry and I can't make it, I'll make sure to get it to you ASAP.<P>Here goes nothin'...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Joe Torma||09-23-2001||01:56 AM||||Careful using Lowes bolts...if they are anything like Home Cheapo bolts, they are sh!t.  Also, if you get nuts with your bolts, make sure they are the special ones that won't back off.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierowrecker||09-23-2001||02:20 AM||||Hey Bryce!<BR>Hope it is going well for you!<BR>Yes, I did burn out the rubber bushings on the car...<BR>Smelly and messy, but I kept a spray bottle of soapy water on hand to keep down the flames...<BR>Once the rubber was out, then I used scewdrivers of different thickness to open up the sleaves...<BR>A propane torch only cost a few bucks and is handy for lighting the BBQ as well as working on the Fiero!<BR>So far, I have done 2 cars with poly, and it gets easier every time...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Nashco||09-23-2001||02:42 AM||||Crap...I just realized while working that I don't have a grease gun!  I was wanting to drive the car tomorrow, but I can't drive with all ungreased balljoints..DOH!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Tigger, you happen to have a grease gun?  I know that zero shops will be open early Sunday morning...what a retard...I forgot to plan for that!<P>I know about using hardware store bolts..this is just temporary.  I did get nyloc nuts, so they have a nylon washer built into them that will not let them spin if they get loose.<P>Well...got the entire right side together using my guess-o-torque arm specs.  I'll be going over everything again after driving, so I'm not too worried about torque specs.  As long as it's too tight, rather than not tight enough, I'll be alright.  Now time to bolt the wheel on and start grinding...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||09-23-2001||07:59 AM||||"Haha!  You can't beat me car, I control your destiny!" said the crazed man after putting the car down on all fours again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Well, she's down on the ground again.  It's been a while, so we'll have to get reacquainted soon.  I got it done...finally...and with 40 dollars of unexpected expenses.  I just can't believe I didn't think about greasing the balljoints!  I'm not sure if I'll be able to make it to the meet today...we'll see.  Hopefully I can find somewhere open today, but I am doubtful.<P>FYI, the grinder worked alright, I just had to jack up the car as high as I could (with my stupid factory jack) so I had enough pivot area for the control arm.  It took a little finessing and weird angles, but I got it.  It was really easy once the disc got smaller in diameter, because I could get into that corner deeper without hitting other things.<P>I think it's time for a nappy now...working all night sucks...but not as bad as studying all night I guess <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tigger||09-23-2001||11:33 AM||||Shoot... I'd let you borrow my grease gun but it didn't work last I tried and haven't taken the time to see what's wrong with it.<P>BTW, won't you need to have an alignment done too?<P>If you won't need the fork, the best day for me to pick it up this week would be Wednesday after 5:00.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Nashco||09-23-2001||12:06 PM||||Damn...I'm gonna call a couple of the cheapy oil change places and see what they'll do for a few bucks.  There ought to be somebody who'll pump those suckers up...  I will need an alignment, but the car will drive without one.  I, obviously, can't test drive it without doing the grease, and I don't want to get an alignment without settling things in a tiny bit.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
